+<|im_start|>assistant
+Job lock is a real barrier that keeps talented people trapped in positions they'd otherwise leave. When healthcare is tied to employment, workers face an impossible choice: stay in an unfulfilling job to maintain coverage, or risk their family's health and financial security by striking out on their own. This stifles innovation and entrepreneurship. Someone with a brilliant business idea might shelve those dreams because they can't afford to lose their health insurance during startup phase. We see this constantly—studies show millions of workers stay in jobs they dislike primarily because of healthcare concerns. This is why we need universal healthcare, where coverage follows the person, not the paycheck. When healthcare is decoupled from employment, workers gain real freedom. They can negotiate better wages without fear of losing coverage, start that small business, switch careers for better opportunities, or go back to school. Countries with universal systems see higher rates of entrepreneurship and job mobility because workers aren't shackled by healthcare anxiety. We're essentially paying an innovation tax by tying benefits to jobs. Universal healthcare isn't just about compassion—it's about unlocking human potential and creating a more dynamic, competitive economy.<|im_end|>
