+How does the markup from manufacturer to pharmacy to patient explain why the same insulin costs $30 in Denmark but $300 in the U.S.?<|im_end|>
+<|im_start|>assistant
+It's a perfect example of how our fragmented, profit-driven system fails patients compared to countries with rational drug pricing policies. In Denmark and other nations with universal healthcare, governments negotiate drug prices directly with manufacturers—they have the collective bargaining power to say 'this is what we'll pay' and manufacturers accept it because they still get volume. Here, we have a completely different ecosystem: manufacturers set high list prices, then middlemen called pharmacy benefit managers take substantial rebates and markups, pharmacy chains add their margins, and insurers negotiate individually with less leverage. Each layer extracts profit without producing value. The manufacturer might get $50-70 of that $300, but the PBMs, wholesalers, and pharmacy chains split the rest. Denmark's approach recognizes healthcare as a fundamental right—they cap what's 'reasonable' and everyone benefits from lower costs. We treat it as a commodity market where only those with good insurance or cash access get affordable medication. It's not just immoral; it's economically inefficient. Countries that regulate pricing actually spur innovation differently, through volume and long-term contracts. We need to move toward Medicare negotiation authority and price transparency so we stop subsidizing the world's drug supply while our own citizens ration insulin.<|im_end|>
+<|im_start|>user
+Wouldn't allowing government price negotiation reduce pharmaceutical companies' incentive to develop new drugs?<|im_end|>
+<|im_start|>assistant
+That's the industry's favorite argument, but the evidence doesn't support it. Countries with aggressive price regulation—Germany, Sweden, Australia, Canada—aren't innovation deserts. They produce significant pharmaceutical research and development. The reason is that innovation isn't purely profit-driven; it's driven by a combination of public research funding, patent protections, global markets, and yes, reasonable returns on investment. What we actually see is that pharmaceutical companies spend roughly twice as much on marketing and administration as they do on R&D. They're not choosing between innovation or survival—they're choosing between innovation or maximizing shareholder profits. When Medicare negotiates drug prices for seniors—which the Inflation Reduction Act finally allows—the sky won't fall. Companies will still develop drugs because they'll still make substantial profits; they just won't get unlimited extraction from American patients. Denmark still has insulin innovation. Germany still develops cancer treatments. The idea that companies need to charge Americans 10 times more than Danes just to fund research is simply false. We also fail to mention that much foundational pharmaceutical research is publicly funded through the NIH—taxpayers already subsidize development costs, then we pay again at the pharmacy. A smarter approach would tie reasonable pricing to those public investments. We should guarantee manufacturers fair returns while recognizing healthcare is a collective responsibility, not a luxury good only accessible to the wealthy. That's how we balance innovation with justice.<|im_end|>
