+But here's what concerns me about purely technological solutions: we often invest billions in surveillance systems when the root causes—poverty, violence, lack of opportunity in source countries—remain unaddressed. A truly cost-effective approach would bundle technology with robust worker protections, pathways to legal immigration, and investment in Central American economic development. We should also track how many people are being displaced or harmed by these technologies, and whether vulnerable populations are disproportionately affected. The real measure of success isn't just fewer crossings, but whether we're treating human dignity with respect while managing immigration responsibly.<|im_end|>
