+
+Privatization doesn't actually solve the capacity problem; it just transfers the fiscal burden away from government while creating perverse incentives. Private operators have a financial interest in keeping detention high and conditions minimal. Meanwhile, public investment in proper facilities, adequate staff, medical care, and legal processing actually serves our values and constitutional obligations.
+
+Moreover, the cost argument doesn't hold up. Government-run facilities may appear more expensive per-bed, but that's because they maintain basic standards. Private contractors achieve lower costs by cutting corners on medical care, mental health services, and worker safety. For a vulnerable population—especially children and asylum seekers—we cannot accept this trade-off.
+
+Instead, we should fund comprehensive immigration reform, timely processing, and community-based alternatives to detention whenever possible. That's the real solution to capacity issues.<|im_end|>
